; metacalc formula drawing for the TI-84 Plus SE. Lays out one statement of the library,
; unfolded, and draws it on the canvas pixel for pixel as the host model does (crates/mcui,
; crates/mcglyph): the tokens of the statement, each the glyph codes of its symbol from the
; library's glyph map, placed in lines by mcglyph's place, drawn with ui_text at x plus the
; token's column on the text row of its line, only on rows inside a window. It also builds the
; statement's subexpression table, the nodes of the model's Expr. Folding, the ellipsis and the
; cursor are not here.
;
; Code runs from flash in bank A and is position dependent. An including file defines FM_EMBEDDED
; first and places the code itself, after the reader (lib/) and the canvas (ui/) it calls, with -I
; at the repository root and at app/src. The canvas's ui_set_font must have been called before
; fm_statement measures a token and before fm_draw draws one.
;
; Memory, placed by app/src/ram_map.inc. FM_RAM is in bank C of the kernel's spare page
; KERNEL_RAM_PAGE, from KERNEL_RAM_HIGH, $1E00 bytes, which the kernel uses only inside a kernel
; call; fm maps the page in every public routine and keeps everything there:
;   +$000 $100  variables (fm/ram.inc)
;   +$100 $100  fm's private stack, FM_STACK_TOP at +$200
;   +$200 $800  FM_GLYPHS, the library's glyph map section as lib_glyphs finds it
;   +$A00 $800  FM_SECOND, the library's shapes section as lib_shapes finds it
;   +$1200 $100 symbol ids of the loaded statement, at most 255
;   +$1300 $100 token widths in pixels
;   +$1400 $100 the columns between a token and the one before it, 1 or 3
;   +$1500 $100 token spacing flags
;   +$1600 $100 FM_FIRST, the first token of each node of the node table, at most 255 nodes
;   +$1700 $100 FM_LAST, the last token of each node
;   +$1800 $100 FM_DEPTH, the depth of each node
;   +$1900 $100 FM_TLEN, FM_TCNT and FM_TSIZE at +$1A00 and +$1B00: what fm_nodes keeps for each
;               node of the stored tree, at most 255 (nodes.inc)
;   +$1C00 $200 FM_PILE, fm_nodes' explicit stack: 255 pairs of two bytes, one for each tree
;               node still open, so a tree of 255 nodes cannot overrun it
; FM_SCRATCH is 256 bytes in bank B, in saveSScreen after UI_VARS, for the buffers the reader's
; accessors write; it holds nothing between calls.
;
; Sessions. Every public routine runs as a session, as the kernel's calls do: it reads the
; interrupt state with ld a,i twice, turns interrupts off, reads port 5, maps the spare page, saves
; the old port 5 and SP in it, switches to FM_STACK and works there, and on every exit restores SP,
; port 5 and the interrupt state. Interrupts stay off for the whole session; the longest one
; measured is fm_nodes of the largest statement of the ring library, 809,934 T-states (135 ms at
; 6 MHz), then fm_open on it, 548,618; fm_draw of a full window takes at most 216,215. The
; reader and the canvas run inside it: lib_read saves the caller's port 5, which is the spare page,
; and puts it back, with no stack instruction in between, and needs its buffer in bank B; the canvas
; touches only bank B, the stack and the font table it was given. A session calls no OS routine.
; Port 7 changes only inside lib_read, which puts it back.
;
; The kernel's contract. The ready mark kv_ready holds READY_MAGIC after a good kernel_init and
; nothing else makes the kernel treat it as set. On entry a session reads the mark: unless it holds
; FM_OWNED, some kernel entry (kernel_init, good or refused, or kernel_reset) has run since the last
; fm session and may have written the high region, so fm is closed: every routine but fm_open then
; returns FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN and draws nothing. Then the session always writes FM_OWNED to the mark,
; so kernel_verify, kernel_crc_begin and kernel_crc_chunk refuse until kernel_init runs again. An fm call between
; kernel_crc_begin and the last kernel_crc_chunk therefore ends the CRC run: the next chunk is
; refused. A caller opens fm again with fm_open, which copies the glyph section afresh; fm_open
; also keeps where the section was and the flash page of the library, and fm_statement returns
; FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN unless the reader's glyph section lies at the same offset, has the same length and
; is in the same flash page. A kernel_reset at the start of an app closes fm.
;
; The test image (crates/mcemu/tests/routines/reader_test.z80) starts at $3000, in page 0, because
; the kernel, the reader, the canvas and fm together are longer than one 16 KB page. That is the
; test's layout and not the app's.
;
; Calling. Status comes back in A, 0 meaning ok, with the zero flag set exactly when A is 0. Every
; routine destroys all registers but IY and keeps the reader's rule for ports 5 and 7 and for the
; interrupt state, which are the caller's again on every exit. The private stack is 256 bytes, FM_STACK
; to FM_STACK_TOP, and the fm variables lie right below it. The tests measure 14 bytes pushed, the
; deepest of any session they run; that is a measurement and not a bound, since the reader alone may
; push 16 bytes below its return address.
;
;   fm_open       in:  the reader open with lib_open
;                 out: A = status. Copies the whole glyph section into FM_GLYPHS, checks that
;                      every entry lies inside it, and marks fm open. Closed until it returns 0.
;                      FM_ERR_CLOSED: the reader is not open. FM_ERR_NO_MAP: the library has no
;                      glyph map. FM_ERR_FONT: its map is for another font. FM_ERR_TOO_BIG: the
;                      section is longer than 2,048 bytes. FM_ERR_BAD: an entry the section
;                      cannot hold. FM_ERR_READ: the reader refused a read.
;   fm_statement  in:  HL = assertion number, E = statement, numbered as lib_tree numbers them:
;                      the essential hypotheses in record order from 0, FFh the conclusion
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of tokens. The tokens are the statement's
;                      symbols as lib_hypothesis and lib_conclusion give them, the typecode
;                      first. FM_ERR_INDEX: no such assertion or statement. FM_ERR_BAD: a
;                      symbol past the glyph map, or a token wider than 255 pixels. A refused
;                      call leaves no statement loaded.
;   fm_rows       in:  D = width in pixels, 0 to 255
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of lines the loaded statement takes. A token
;                      wider than the width sits alone on its line.
;   fm_draw       in:  HL = text row of the statement's first line, signed, -16384 to 16383;
;                      D = width in pixels; E = x of the statement's first column; B = the first
;                      text row of the window, C = one past its last
;                 out: A = status. Draws each line whose row is in the window, with ui_text,
;                      and nothing else.
;   fm_nodes      in:  nothing; the statement loaded by fm_statement
;                 out: A = status, B = the number of nodes. Builds the node table of the loaded
;                      statement, which is the model's Expr for it: the nodes in preorder, node 0
;                      the whole statement, the typecode token 0 of node 0 and of no other. Node
;                      n spans tokens FM_FIRST[n] to FM_LAST[n], at depth FM_DEPTH[n]; the parent
;                      is the nearest earlier node one level up and is not stored. A tree node whose
;                      only part is a child has no node of its own: the child takes its place at
;                      its depth. A statement the library has no tree for is one node over every
;                      token, depth 0, as is every statement of a library with no trees. The
;                      table is built once for a loaded statement and a second call returns at
;                      once; fm_statement marks it not built, and a refused call leaves it not
;                      built, the statement loaded. FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN: fm closed or another library
;                      open. FM_ERR_INDEX: no statement loaded. FM_ERR_READ: the reader refused a
;                      read. FM_ERR_TREE: the tree ends early or holds bytes after its root, a
;                      variable that is not the token at its place, or tokens that do not add up
;                      to the statement's. FM_ERR_LIMIT: more than 255 tree nodes, which also
;                      bounds the table nodes, the depth and the stack at 255.
; Other statuses: FM_ERR_NOT_OPEN, from fm_statement, fm_rows and fm_draw.
; fm_open also copies the shapes section into FM_SECOND and checks it, the count against the
; reader's syntax count, the offset table, every item's place and length and each child of its
; axiom's arity once; FM_ERR_TOO_BIG for a section over 2,048 bytes and FM_ERR_BAD for what fails
; the checks, with the glyph map's. A library with neither trees nor shapes opens. A library with
; a glyph map and damaged shapes does not.

#ifndef FM_EMBEDDED
.org $4000
#endif

#include "fm/ram.inc"
#include "fm/session.inc"
#include "fm/open.inc"
#include "fm/shapes.inc"
#include "fm/statement.inc"
#include "fm/layout.inc"
#include "fm/nodes.inc"
